WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.966
Inputs arrive through many paths: HTTP
requests, RPC calls, messages,

2
00:00:04.046 --> 00:00:08.264
files, environment variables, webhooks,
administrative tools, and scheduled jobs.

3
00:00:08.344 --> 00:00:12.215
A secure Go service treats these inputs as
external until they have been parsed,

4
00:00:12.295 --> 00:00:15.010
validated, and normalized into safer
internal models.

5
00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.267
Validation should happen before business
logic depends on data.

6
00:00:18.347 --> 00:00:21.698
In Go, this often means decoding into a
request-specific structure,

7
00:00:21.778 --> 00:00:24.031
checking required fields, enforcing
limits,

8
00:00:24.111 --> 00:00:28.098
and then creating an internal command or
query type that represents the operation

9
00:00:28.178 --> 00:00:29.853
the service is willing to perform.

10
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:34.035
The handler should not pass raw, loosely
understood data deep into the application.

11
00:00:34.115 --> 00:00:38.043
Good validation covers structure, type,
size, allowed values, business rules,

12
00:00:38.123 --> 00:00:38.759
and context.

13
00:00:38.839 --> 00:00:40.572
A string may be present but too long.

14
00:00:40.652 --> 00:00:44.292
A numeric value may be valid as a number
but invalid for the current account.

15
00:00:44.372 --> 00:00:47.780
A field may be allowed for one role but
ignored or rejected for another.

16
00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.442
Architecture should provide a consistent
place to make those decisions.

17
00:00:51.522 --> 00:00:55.393
Normalization matters because external
formats are often shaped for callers,

18
00:00:55.473 --> 00:00:56.975
not for internal correctness.

19
00:00:57.055 --> 00:01:00.868
The service may need to trim or reject
ambiguous values, resolve identifiers,

20
00:01:00.948 --> 00:01:05.050
apply safe defaults intentionally, or
convert external names into internal enums.

21
00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:09.290
These choices should be visible and
repeatable, not hidden in scattered helper code.

22
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.161
Output design is equally important.

23
00:01:11.241 --> 00:01:13.725
Services should return only what the
caller needs.

24
00:01:13.805 --> 00:01:16.809
Internal database models, audit fields,
operational flags,

25
00:01:16.889 --> 00:01:20.529
and sensitive attributes should not
automatically become API responses.

26
00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:24.826
Response structures should be deliberately
shaped so future developers can see which

27
00:01:24.906 --> 00:01:26.177
fields are safe to expose.

28
00:01:26.257 --> 00:01:30.070
Consistent validation and serialization
patterns make the service easier to

29
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:30.670
maintain.

30
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.194
When new endpoints are added,

31
00:01:32.274 --> 00:01:34.990
engineers should be able to follow an
established path:

32
00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.132
accept external input, validate it, map it
to internal intent,

33
00:01:38.212 --> 00:01:40.580
enforce authorization, perform the
operation,

34
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.740
and serialize only the approved response.
